How to use mixpost in your website

1. Use a Laravel app as the host
   Your real app is the Laravel project in backend, not the mixpost folder.

2. Install mixpost into that Laravel app
   You can add it via Composer like a normal package.

If you want to use the local folder in this repo, add this to composer.json:

Then run from backend:

This tells Composer to use the local mixpost package source.

3. Publish assets and migrations
   After installation, you normally run:

(The exact publish tags depend on the package, but package docs usually tell you.)

4. Access the Mixpost UI
   Once installed into Laravel, Mixpost routes are available under:

http://<your-backend-host>/mixpost
Example:

http://localhost:8000/mixpost if using php artisan serve
http://localhost:8085/mixpost if using Docker for the backend
What it means in this repo
Right now, mixpost is a package source folder. It is not the website root because:

it has no artisan
it has no Laravel app structure
it has its own composer.json for package metadata
So the correct way to “use it in our website” is:

host it inside the backend Laravel app
install it as a package
then open mixpost on that Laravel app URL
